#' Let's get started with R!
#' We'll go over the following topics today:
#' - Vectors
#' - Data Frames
#' - Tables
#' - Basic Functions
#' - Subsetting
#' - Apply/Map Functions

#' Vectors
#' In R, a vector is data structure that represents an ordered collection of
#' elements of the *same* data type. Vectors are be *one-dimensional*, meaning
#' they have a single dimension (rows or columns), and they are used to store
#' and manipulate sequences of values.

# An example of a vector of text
days <- c("Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday", "Saturday", "Sunday")
days

# Numerical Vectors NOTE: The : is a colon operator, in this case it means 1 to 18
age <- 1:18
age

#' Data Frames
#' In R, a dataframe is a two-dimensional, tabular data structure similar to a
#' spreadsheet. It is one of the most commonly used and versatile data
#' structures in R for handling and analyzing data. Dataframes can store
#' different types of data (numeric, character, logical) in columns, and each
#' column can be considered as a vector. Datafames can be created in several
#' ways in R, but the most common way is to read in a CSV file either from a
#' website or from your local computer.
moody <- read.csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dev7796/data101_tutorial/main/files/dataset/moody2022.csv")
head(moody)

#' Getting subsets of a dataframe
#' What if we want to look at a particular row or column in the dataframe? Well
#' that's where data subseting comes in. We can use the square brackets to
#' capture a subset of the data. This first example gets the fifth row of the
#' moody dataframe.
moody[5, ]

# For a column, we can put the number of the column on the other side
moody[, 1]

# To get a set of rows or columns, we use the colon operator!
moody[1:10, 1]

# Give me rows 1-5 and columns 1 and 3 of moody
moody[1:5, 1:3]

#' Contingency Table
#' Imagine this scenario: you are a professor and you want to know how many
#' students are failing your class? How can we get a count of the number of
#' students who fall into each category? Well, we can use a contingency table!
#' In statistical analysis, a contingency table displays the frequency
#' distribution of two categorical variables. The table function in R is
#' commonly used to create contingency tables.
gpa <- table(moody$Grade)
gpa

#' What if we want to look at the number of students who failed in each major
#' we can create a table for that as well!
table(moody$Major, moody$Grade)

#' Basic Functions
#' R has a lot of built in functions that we can use to manipulate our data.
#' These are some of the most common ones:
mean(moody$GPA)
length(moody$Major) # How can we get the number of unique majors?
max(moody$GPA)
min(moody$GPA)
sd(moody$GPA)

#' Filters
#' Sometimes we want to filter our data based on a certain condition. For
#' example, what if we want to look at the students who are majoring in Stats?
#' What about students who have a score greater than 85 and a grade A?
moody[moody$Major == "Statistics", ]
moody[moody$Score > 85 & moody$Grade == "A", ]

#' Subsetting Columns
#' Sometimes we want to remove columns from our dataframe. We can do this using
#' the subset function. Let's say we want to remove the first column of the
#' dataframe.
colnames(moody)

# Removing the first column why do we use `c` here?
moody3 <- subset(moody, select = -c(1))
head(moody3)

# Subset of Rows and Columns with a filter
moody1 <- subset(moody, select = c(1:3), Major == "Statistics")
colnames(moody1)

# Notice that only 3 columns are remaining
dim(moody1)

#' Apply Functions
#' Say we want to apply a function to each row or column of a dataframe.
#' For example, what if we want to get the mean of each group? We can use the
#' `tapply()` function to do this. For example, the below line first filters
#' dataframe to only include students who are Freshman, then it gets the mean
#' GPA of those students. The second parameter here specifies the grouping in
#' this situation we want to group the means by the letter grade.
tapply(moody[moody$Seniority == "Freshman", ]$Score, moody[moody$Seniority == "Freshman", ]$Grade, mean)

# Here's another example
values <- c(12, 15, 8, 10, 14, 9)
groups <- c("A", "B", "A", "B", "A", "B")
mean_by_group <- tapply(values, groups, mean)
mean_by_group

# Here's some other common functions
colnames(moody)

summary(moody)

unique(moody$Major)

min(moody[moody$Grade == "B", ]$Score)

max(moody[moody$Grade == "A", ]$Score)

tapply(moody[moody$Grade == "B", ]$Score, moody[moody$Grade == "B", ]$Major, min)

#' The Cut Function
#' The cut function is used to create bins (groups) for continuous variables.
#' For example, we want to categorize the students based on their score. We can
#' specify our `breaks` and our `labels` to create the bins.
moody$ScoreIntervals <- cut(moody$Score, breaks = c(0, 50, 75, 90, 100), labels = c("Low", "Medium", "Good", "Excellent"))
table(moody$ScoreIntervals)

#' Problem Solving
#' Let's see if a student's major affect the grade averages and cutoff points!
cat("Grade Averages in Psychology ")
moodyP <- moody[moody$Major == "Psychology", ]
tapply(moodyP$Score, moodyP$Grade, mean)

cat("Grade Averages in Computer Science ")
moodyCS <- moody[moody$Major == "CS", ]
tapply(moodyCS$Score, moodyCS$Grade, mean)

cat("Grade Averages in Statistics")
moodyStat <- moody[moody$Major == "Statistics", ]
tapply(moodyStat$Score, moodyStat$Grade, mean)

cat("Grade Averages in Economics")
moodyEcon <- moody[moody$Major == "Economics", ]
tapply(moodyEcon$Score, moodyEcon$Grade, mean)


movie_data <- read.csv("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dev7796/data101_tutorial/main/files/dataset/Movies2022F-4.csv")
head(movie_data)

# Summary of the dataset.
summary(movie_data)

# Finding the unique values in content, genre, gross and budget.(Categorical attributes)
unique(movie_data$content)
unique(movie_data$genre)
unique(movie_data$Gross)
unique(movie_data$Budget)

#' Table for Genre vs Budget. Family movies are all high budget movies.
#' What if I wanted to see the budget of the movie vs the gross amount?
table(movie_data$genre, movie_data$Budget)

#' Here we use the tapply function in R to calculate the 
#' mean IMDb score for movies, grouped by 
#' their budget levels. Notice that we use a filter to specify
#' only movies that performed well at the box office. Why do you 
#' think movies with a low budget performed better? Selection bias?
#' Do you think it's worth it to see the number of movies in each category?
tapply(movie_data$imdb_score, movie_data$Budget, mean)
tapply(movie_data[movie_data$Gross=="High", ]$imdb_score, movie_data[movie_data$Gross=="High", ]$Budget, mean)

# Gross-wise Max imdb score for Action movies from UK. Values range in between 8.2 to 8.5
tapply(movie_data[movie_data$country=="UK" & movie_data$genre=="Action", ]$imdb_score, movie_data[movie_data$country=="UK" & movie_data$genre=="Action", ]$Gross, max)

#' Bar Plot
#' Bar plots are usually used for displaying the distribution of categorical data. 
#' The x-axis represents different categories, and the y-axis represents the frequency, 
#' count, or some other aggregated measure associated with each category. Here we look
#' at the counts of each genre present within our data, notice the filter!
genre_counts <- table(movie_data[movie_data$imdb_score>6.5, ]$genre)
genre_counts
barplot(genre_counts, main="Popular Movie Genres", xlab="Genre", ylab="Count", col="red")

#' Now we modify the filter to look at the number of movies in each genre
#' that performed well at the box office.
genre_counts1 <- table(movie_data[movie_data$Gross=="High", ]$genre)
genre_counts1
barplot(genre_counts1, main="High Grossing Movie Genres", xlab="Genre", ylab="Count", col="darkgreen")

#' Lastly we modify the bar chart to look at the comparision of box office 
#' performance between movies made in the states vs not. 
gross_counts <- table(movie_data$country=="USA", movie_data$Gross)
gross_counts

new_row_names <- c("Not American", "American")
rownames(gross_counts) <- new_row_names
gross_counts

barplot(gross_counts, main="Gross count for USA", xlab="Gross", ylab="Count", col = c("lightblue", "red"), beside=TRUE, legend=TRUE)

#' Box Plot
#' A boxplot, also known as a box-and-whisker plot, is a graphical representation 
#' that provides a summary of the distribution of a set of data. It displays key 
#' statistics such as the minimum, first quartile (Q1), median (Q2), third quartile (Q3), 
#' and maximum. Additionally, it helps identify any potential outliers in the dataset.
#' In this example we look at the subset of the dataframe where the movie's IMDB score
#' is greater than 7 and we only pick the third column of the data
high_imdb_movies <- subset(movie_data, movie_data$imdb_score >= 7, select = c(3))
boxplot(high_imdb_movies, main="IMDb Score Distribution for High-Rated Movies", ylab="IMDb Score", col="orange")

#' Mosaic Plot
#' A mosaic plot is a graphical method for visualizing the relationship between two or more 
#' categorical variables. It is a type of segmented bar chart that displays the relative 
#' proportions of categories within each level of one or more categorical variables. 
#' Mosaic plots are particularly useful for understanding the association and distribution 
#' of categorical variables in contingency tables.
colors<- c('red', 'blue', 'cyan')
mosaicplot(movie_data$Gross~movie_data$Budget, xlab = 'Gross', ylab ='Budget', main="Mosiac Plot of Gross vs Budget", col=colors, border="black")

# Mosaic Plot for content and genre.
colors1 <- c('red', 'blue', 'cyan', 'yellow', 'green', 'orange')
mosaicplot(movie_data$content~movie_data$genre, xlab='Content', ylab ='Genre', main="Mosiac Plot of Content vs Genre", col=colors1, border="black")

# Generate random data points
# sample() generates points randomly given the range.
x <- sample(1:100, 50) 
y <- sample(1:100, 50)

#' Scatter plot
#' A scatterplot is a graphical representation of the relationship between two continuous variables. 
#' It displays individual data points on a two-dimensional plane, with one variable on the x-axis 
#' and another on the y-axis. Each point on the plot represents a pair of values for the two variables.
#' NOTE: So far we've mainly been working with categorical data, that's why we'll have to use a different 
#' dataset for scatterplots
plot(x, y, main ="Scatter Plot of Random Numbers", xlab ="X-axis", ylab ="Y-axis", col="blue")

